        <section class="container">
            <div class="essay essay--text-left">
                <div class="essay__text reveal">
                    <h2 class="essay__heading">Why Traditional Finnish Saunas?</h2>
                    <p>Ours are built the way a house is built. A framed envelope, insulated to R-13 or better, sealed behind a vapour barrier, clad in Western Red Cedar inside and metal outside. That construction is what makes proper löyly possible at 80 to 100°C, holds the heat once it is there instead of pouring it into the yard, and keeps the whole thing standing outdoors for twenty-five years and more.</p>
                    <p>The usual alternatives cannot do that. A barrel sauna is single-wall stave construction with no insulation at all, which makes real heat hard to reach and quick to lose, and five to ten years is a typical life outdoors. An infrared cabin is not a sauna: it warms your body rather than the air, tops out around 60°C, and cannot make steam.</p>
                    <p class="essay__link"><a href="/faq/" class="link--accent">The full comparison →</a></p>
                </div>
                <div class="essay__plate reveal">
